1
00:00:00,169 --> 00:00:02,950
Suppose you manage a factory that makes widgets.
假设你管理着一家制造小部件的工厂。

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:07,332
You've got a spinning thing over there, you've got an up and downy thing over here.
那边有个旋转的东西，这边有个上下移动的东西。

3
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:17,397
It's a complex setup that produces a lot of vibrations, so you're quite reasonably concerned that one day your factory could shake itself to bits.
这是一个复杂的设置，会产生很多振动，所以你很合理地担心有一天你的工厂可能会自己震碎。

4
00:00:17,817 --> 00:00:29,622
Like, a small vibration here may cause a bolt to come loose, which causes even more vibrations, that leads to metal fatigue over here, which leads to even larger vibrations.
比如，这里的微小振动可能会导致螺栓松动，进而引发更多的振动，导致这里的金属疲劳，进而引发更大的振动。

5
00:00:29,982 --> 00:00:31,023
It's a snowball effect.
这是一个滚雪球效应。

6
00:00:31,243 --> 00:00:37,686
So you want to catch the vibrations early before they become a problem, before they're even visible to the human eye.
所以你想在振动成为问题之前，甚至在它们肉眼可见之前，尽早捕捉到它们。

7
00:00:38,186 --> 00:00:39,166
So what do you do?
那么你该怎么做呢？

8
00:00:39,387 --> 00:00:42,928
Well, you could put some sensors on things that you're worried about.
嗯，你可以在你担心的东西上安装一些传感器。

9
00:00:43,028 --> 00:00:48,970
So maybe you put an accelerometer on this pipe over here that you're worried might be moving up and down too much.
所以也许你在这根你担心可能上下移动过多的管道上安装了一个加速度计。

10
00:00:49,531 --> 00:00:57,694
And that sensor supplies you with data and you plot a graph and you can see it moving up and down and it's within limits and you can monitor it and it's fine.
那个传感器会给你提供数据，你可以绘制一个图表，看到它上下移动，并且在限制范围内，你可以监控它，一切正常。

11
00:00:58,414 --> 00:01:04,676
But what if there's a part of the factory you forget to put a sensor on because you don't think it's a concern?
但如果工厂的某个部分你忘记安装传感器了，因为你认为它不是问题呢？

12
00:01:05,477 --> 00:01:14,080
What if you could take millions of sensors and just throw them across your factory and capture everything?
如果你能把数百万个传感器扔到你的工厂里，捕捉到一切呢？

13
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:20,161
It turns out you can, and in fact, you carry a set of these sensors with you all the time.
事实证明你可以，而且实际上，你一直随身携带着这些传感器。

14
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:24,242
I'm talking about your phone, specifically the camera in your phone.
我说的是你的手机，特别是你手机里的摄像头。

15
00:01:24,642 --> 00:01:33,524
That chip that gathers light for the picture, the CMOS chip, is made of millions of individual sensors, one for each pixel.
那个为照片收集光线的芯片，CMOS芯片，是由数百万个独立的传感器组成的，每个像素一个。

16
00:01:33,684 --> 00:01:36,285
Well, I suppose three for each pixel, actually.
嗯，实际上，每个像素可能有三个。

17
00:01:36,565 --> 00:01:41,834
In reality, you're probably going to want to use something better than your camera phone, something industrial with a global shutter.
实际上，你可能想使用比手机摄像头更好的东西，比如带有全局快门的工业相机。

18
00:01:41,954 --> 00:01:45,459
So you get a camera and you take some videos of your factory.
所以你拿了一台相机，拍摄了一些工厂的视频。

19
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:47,843
Some really wide shots to begin with.
一开始拍摄了一些非常宽广的镜头。

20
00:01:48,304 --> 00:01:50,387
Make sure you capture absolutely everything.
确保捕捉到绝对一切。

21
00:01:51,098 --> 00:02:00,761
But if what you're looking for is vibrations that are so small, they're not perceptible with human vision, then how can a camera see these vibrations?
但如果你要找的是那些小到人眼无法察觉的振动，那么相机如何能看到这些振动呢？

22
00:02:01,121 --> 00:02:03,782
That's where motion amplification comes in.
这就是运动放大的用武之地。

23
00:02:04,022 --> 00:02:07,863
In fact, all the footage I've shown you so far is motion amplified.
事实上，到目前为止我给你展示的所有镜头都是经过运动放大的。

24
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:13,865
For example, here you can see side-by-side the original footage and the motion amplified footage.
例如，这里你可以看到并排的原版镜头和经过运动放大的镜头。

25
00:02:14,265 --> 00:02:18,686
The original footage doesn't look like it's moving at all, but there is a subtle movement information in there
原版镜头看起来完全没有移动，但里面确实有一些微妙的运动信息。

26
00:02:18,906 --> 00:02:20,667
that's been amplified on the other side.
在另一边已经被放大了。

27
00:02:20,947 --> 00:02:31,352
In fact, an original video can contain sub-pixel movements and still have that information amplified, so you don't even have a hard edge sweeping across several pixels.
事实上，原始视频可能包含亚像素级别的移动，并且仍然可以放大这些信息，因此你甚至不会看到硬边缘扫过多个像素。

28
00:02:31,793 --> 00:02:42,158
Instead, you've got a line of pixels that are getting darker while the neighboring pixels are getting lighter, and you can interpret that information as movement and amplify it.
相反，你看到的是一条像素变得更暗，而相邻像素变得更亮，你可以将这些信息解释为移动并进行放大。

29
00:02:42,638 --> 00:02:47,739
It's incredible, really, especially when you consider that you're extracting this information against the background of noise.
这真的很不可思议，尤其是当你考虑到你是在噪声的背景下提取这些信息时。

30
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:57,601
All digital images and videos contain a bit of noise, which is a fancy way of saying random fluctuations in brightness for individual pixels.
所有数字图像和视频都包含一些噪声，这是一种花哨的说法，指的是单个像素亮度的随机波动。

31
00:02:57,881 --> 00:03:04,742
I spoke to Jeff Hay at RDA Technologies, who created a motion amplification system that's used in industrial settings.
我与RDA Technologies的Jeff Hay进行了交谈，他创建了一个用于工业环境的运动放大系统。

32
00:03:04,982 --> 00:03:05,882
I started in astronomy.
我最初是在天文学领域开始的。

33
00:03:05,902 --> 00:03:09,803
I thought I was going to be an astronomer, but I think I had a
我曾以为自己会成为一名天文学家，但我想我

34
00:03:11,197 --> 00:03:15,264
a conversation with my advisor that was about being gainfully employed.
和我导师的一次关于就业的对话。

35
00:03:15,685 --> 00:03:21,114
You know, remarkably, the problem sets kind of the same, you know, I mean, you've got your you're using the camera.
你知道，非常有趣的是，问题集其实差不多，我的意思是，你使用相机。

36
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:27,774
to figure out something about some object that's, you know, kind of at a distance.
来弄清楚某个远处的物体的一些情况。

37
00:03:28,274 --> 00:03:39,381
The other hobby that I had, I was the photo editor for my university newspaper, and so I've got a photography background as well, so all these things sort of just started to come together, you know, it's no secret where I ended up.
我还有一个爱好，我是大学校报的摄影编辑，所以我也有摄影背景，所以所有这些事情都开始融合在一起，你知道，我最终的去向并不神秘。

38
00:03:39,522 --> 00:03:47,347
Even a megapixel camera, which is by today's standards kind of small in resolution, that has a million sensors in it.
即使是百万像素的相机，按照今天的标准，分辨率算是比较小的，但它也有一百万个传感器。

39
00:03:47,567 --> 00:03:47,827
You can
你可以

40
00:03:48,274 --> 00:03:52,056
blanket an object with, you know, sensors and measure everywhere.
用传感器覆盖一个物体并进行全方位测量。

41
00:03:52,256 --> 00:03:55,618
When you have sort of like a motor and a pump, you know, they're connected.
当你有一个电机和泵时，它们是连接在一起的。

42
00:03:55,638 --> 00:04:01,120
The shafts are connected at something that they call coupling, which is just essentially connecting those two pieces of equipment.
轴通过所谓的联轴器连接，这本质上就是将这两台设备连接在一起。

43
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:09,124
And if one side, if the, if, for example, the pump is going up while the motor is going down, that's misalignment and that's bad.
如果一边，比如说，泵在上升而电机在下降，那就是不对齐，这是不好的。

44
00:04:10,343 --> 00:04:15,127
Lots of times if things are moving together, that's some indication that that's good, that's all right.
很多时候，如果它们一起移动，那是一个好的迹象，那是正常的。

45
00:04:15,447 --> 00:04:19,892
And sometimes when they're opposing each other, that's where the problems come in.
而有时当它们相互对立时，问题就出现了。

46
00:04:20,132 --> 00:04:25,637
If you've got a 50 frames per second camera and you've got something that's vibrating 50 times a second, you may not pick it up.
如果你有一个每秒50帧的相机，而某物正在以每秒50次的频率振动，你可能无法捕捉到它。

47
00:04:25,697 --> 00:04:27,258
Do you have to worry about that sort of thing?
你需要担心这种事情吗？

48
00:04:27,979 --> 00:04:28,580
Yeah, yeah.
是的，是的。

49
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:35,486
So usually the rule of thumb is you have to sample, you have to take data at a rate twice the frequency you're interested in.
所以通常的经验法则是，你必须以你感兴趣的频率的两倍速率采样数据。

50
00:04:35,615 --> 00:04:37,995
With this system, you can look at the raw data as well.
有了这个系统，你还可以查看原始数据。

51
00:04:38,035 --> 00:04:40,796
Like you can say, look, tell me what's going on with these pixels here.
就像你可以说的，看，告诉我这些像素这里发生了什么。

52
00:04:40,996 --> 00:04:42,736
I'm interested in the vibrations at that point.
我对那个点的振动感兴趣。

53
00:04:42,976 --> 00:04:45,577
And you can see a nice graph and that's really important.
你可以看到一个漂亮的图表，这真的很重要。

54
00:04:45,877 --> 00:05:02,220
But I'd also say that this summarized visual representation that's so easily digested by the human visual cortex is also really important because human brains are better than computers at certain tasks.
但我也想说，这种由人类视觉皮层如此容易消化的总结性视觉表示也非常重要，因为人类大脑在某些任务上比计算机更好。

55
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:11,191
Maybe not forever, but it's true for now, which is why we use human brains to train artificial intelligence in things like computer vision.
也许不会永远，但目前确实如此，这就是为什么我们使用人类大脑来训练像计算机视觉这样的人工智能。

56
00:05:11,331 --> 00:05:19,021
This is a bit of a diversion, but when you log onto a website and you have to prove that you're human and not a robot, you fill in a capture.
这是一个小小的偏离，但当你登录一个网站并必须证明你是人类而不是机器人时，你填写了一个验证码。

57
00:05:19,481 --> 00:05:26,483
And in the old days that used to be two words that were garbled in some way and you had to type out what the words were.
在过去，那通常是两个以某种方式扭曲的单词，你必须输入这些单词是什么。

58
00:05:26,864 --> 00:05:30,645
An easy task for human vision, a hard task for computers.
对人类视觉来说这是一个简单的任务，对计算机来说却很难。

59
00:05:30,945 --> 00:05:34,686
But actually only one of those words was to test whether you were human.
但实际上，这些单词中只有一个是为了测试你是否是人类。

60
00:05:35,006 --> 00:05:41,868
The other word was something that Google was struggling with as part of their book digitization project.
另一个单词是谷歌在他们的图书数字化项目中遇到困难的部分。

61
00:05:41,888 --> 00:05:44,149
So here's a word we don't recognize in a book.
所以这里有一个我们在书中不认识的单词。

62
00:05:44,409 --> 00:05:46,250
We're gonna outsource it to a human
我们打算通过验证码过程将其外包给人类。

63
00:05:46,670 --> 00:05:48,691
through the capture process.
而且这不仅帮助了那本书中的那个单词，还训练了AI在未来能够识别那种单词。

64
00:05:48,911 --> 00:05:57,633
And it doesn't just help with that one word in that one book, it also trains the AI to be able to recognize that kind of word in the future.
通过这种方式将单词识别外包给人类，谷歌能够快速完成他们的图书数字化项目，这就是为什么你不再看到这些单词验证码了。

65
00:05:57,893 --> 00:06:07,515
By outsourcing word recognition to humans in this way, Google were able to blaze through their book digitization project, which is why you don't see these word captures anymore.
那么你现在看到了什么？

66
00:06:08,095 --> 00:06:09,276
So what do you see now?
嗯，有一段时间，如果你还记得，是数字，它会是一个看起来像模糊的前门照片的数字。

67
00:06:09,356 --> 00:06:19,866
Well, for a while, it was numbers, if you remember, and it would be a picture of a number that looks suspiciously like a blurry photograph of a front door.
猜猜看？

68
00:06:20,367 --> 00:06:21,307
And guess what?
那是在帮助谷歌地图通过从谷歌街景拍摄的照片来识别地址。

69
00:06:21,368 --> 00:06:27,854
That was helping Google Maps to identify addresses through pictures taken from Google Street View.
这些天，验证码任务通常涉及点击所有包含路标或点击所有包含公交车的图像。

70
00:06:28,374 --> 00:06:36,802
These days, the capture task tends to involve something like click on all the images that contain a road sign or click on all the images that contain a bus.
猜猜看？

71
00:06:37,303 --> 00:06:38,023
And guess what?
那是因为谷歌的母公司有一个自动驾驶汽车项目，他们需要人类大脑来帮助他们训练人工智能识别路标和公交车等事物。

72
00:06:38,143 --> 00:06:51,196
That's because Google's parent company has an autonomous vehicle project and they need human brains to help them train their artificial intelligence to recognize things like road signs and buses.
有趣的是，这些验证码是实时的。

73
00:06:51,716 --> 00:06:54,639
Interestingly, those captures are real-time.
所以如果你被要求识别一个路标，那是因为在那一刻有一辆车在街上需要知道那是一个路标。

74
00:06:54,719 --> 00:07:03,869
So if you're asked to identify a road sign, for example, that's because there's a car out there on the street at that moment that needs to know that that's a road sign.
所以如果你看到了，你需要迅速行动，否则可能会有人受伤。

75
00:07:03,949 --> 00:07:07,853
So if you see that, you need to act quickly, otherwise someone could get hurt.
所以如果你看到了，你需要迅速行动，否则可能会有人受伤。

76
00:07:08,194 --> 00:07:09,355
That last bit isn't true.
最后一点并不正确。

77
00:07:10,736 --> 00:07:11,497
Or is it?
还是说，确实如此？

78
00:07:11,517 --> 00:07:13,199
It isn't.
并非如此。

79
00:07:14,266 --> 00:07:27,431
But anyway, the point is, if you can take your data and turn it into something visual, you might be able to outsource some of the processing of that data to a human brain that can produce remarkable insights without even breaking a sweat.
但无论如何，关键在于，如果你能把数据转化为视觉形式，你或许可以将部分数据处理工作外包给人类大脑，而大脑能在不费吹灰之力的情况下产生惊人的洞察力。

80
00:07:27,871 --> 00:07:35,893
And by the way, having this visual representation really helps when you're trying to persuade superiors to spend some money.
顺便说一句，这种视觉呈现方式在说服上级花钱时确实非常有帮助。

81
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:42,876
So if you go to somebody and you show them a plot on a graph and you say, man, that machine's gonna come apart,
所以，如果你去找某人，给他们看一张图表上的曲线，并说，那台机器就要散架了，

82
00:07:44,037 --> 00:07:54,843
That's a hard, you know, but if you can show them that it's lifting up off the ground or it's cracked or, you know, that's kind of an element to it as well.
这很难让人信服，但如果你能展示出它正在从地面升起或已经裂开，那就会更有说服力。

83
00:07:55,183 --> 00:07:59,185
You talked about having, you've got some footage of engines and things like that.
你提到过，你有一些发动机之类的视频素材。

84
00:07:59,545 --> 00:08:04,268
We've got, yeah, we've got car engines.
是的，我们有汽车发动机的视频。

85
00:08:04,508 --> 00:08:08,170
We've got some more common, you know, sort of everyday stuff.
还有一些更常见的日常物品。

86
00:08:08,975 --> 00:08:09,355
that we could do.
我们可以处理这些。

87
00:08:09,436 --> 00:08:11,677
I think it would be a little bit more universal to people.
我认为这对大多数人来说会更普遍易懂。

88
00:08:11,937 --> 00:08:22,786
Geoff is understandably reluctant to talk about the finer technical details of how the technology works, but I really wanted to see if I could create a motion amplified video of my own.
杰夫自然不愿透露这项技术如何运作的细节，但我真的很想尝试自己制作一段运动放大的视频。

89
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:26,929
I did find some research from scientists at MIT
我确实找到了一些来自麻省理工学院科学家的研究。

90
00:08:27,649 --> 00:08:30,210
They're calling it video magnification.
他们称之为视频放大。

91
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:32,611
It's the same kind of idea.
这是一个类似的概念。

92
00:08:32,991 --> 00:08:34,912
You can amplify motion in a video.
你可以在视频中放大运动。

93
00:08:34,952 --> 00:08:37,553
You can also amplify color change in a video.
你也可以在视频中放大颜色变化。

94
00:08:37,673 --> 00:08:38,433
Let me show you what I mean.
让我给你看看我的意思。

95
00:08:38,853 --> 00:08:40,894
This is my wrist.
这是我的手腕。

96
00:08:40,914 --> 00:08:46,996
You can just about see a pulse there, but look, here it is with motion amplification applied.
你几乎可以看到脉搏，但看，这里应用了运动放大技术。

97
00:08:47,176 --> 00:08:49,097
I'll leave some links to their research in the description.
我会在描述中留下一些他们研究的链接。

98
00:08:49,437 --> 00:08:50,957
They've got software you can download.
他们有你可以下载的软件。

99
00:08:50,977 --> 00:08:55,519
They've even got an online system where you can upload video and play with the algorithm.
他们甚至有一个在线系统，你可以上传视频并使用算法进行操作。

100
00:08:55,839 --> 00:08:56,619
It's really cool.
真的很酷。

101
00:08:56,739 --> 00:08:59,701
You can even detect pulse through color amplification.
你甚至可以通过颜色放大来检测脉搏。

102
00:09:00,221 --> 00:09:05,824
The color of my face is changing very, very slightly with each beat of my heart.
我的面部颜色随着每次心跳而发生非常微妙的变化。

103
00:09:06,064 --> 00:09:09,085
You can't see it with the naked eye, but here it is amplified.
肉眼无法察觉，但在这里被放大了。

104
00:09:09,105 --> 00:09:12,026
And then I blink.
然后我眨眼了。

105
00:09:13,287 --> 00:09:16,048
Actually, you can detect pulse just from me standing still.
实际上，仅凭我站立不动就能检测到脉搏。

106
00:09:16,428 --> 00:09:22,351
Here I'm trying to be as still as possible, but every time blood is pumped through my neck, my head wobbles slightly.
我尽量保持静止，但每次血液流经我的颈部时，我的头部都会轻微晃动。

107
00:09:22,851 --> 00:09:24,072
Here it is amplified.
这里被放大了。

108
00:09:26,636 --> 00:09:38,407
There was a lot more motion in my body than just the wobbling of my head due to my pulse, but the algorithm that generates the resulting video is really clever and you can filter by frequency.
我的身体中除了因脉搏引起的头部晃动外，还有更多运动，但生成结果视频的算法非常聪明，你可以按频率进行过滤。

109
